---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Audit log mode · enprot">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-3">Audit log mode</h1>
  <p class="text-enprot-muted mb-6">Append-only signed event stream. <code>tail -f</code> for cryptographic logs.</p>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Scenario</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">
      A compliance team needs a tamper-evident access log. Every login, every deployment, every
      configuration change. The log must be verifiable by auditors weeks later, and any tampering
      must be detectable down to the individual event.
    </p>
    <p>
      Audit log mode maps directly onto Stage 1 chain anchors: each event is its own anchor,
      signed by the logger's key.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Setup</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code># Generate the auditor's signing key (once)
enprot keygen ed25519 --out-priv auditor.pem --out-pub auditor.pub

# Pin the fingerprint in your verification policy
AUDITOR_FP=$(enprot fingerprint auditor.pub)
echo "Trust root: $AUDITOR_FP"</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Streaming events</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code># Pipe events in; each line becomes a chain anchor
(echo "user=alice action=login    ts=$(date -Iseconds)"
 echo "user=bob   action=deploy   ts=$(date -Iseconds)"
 echo "user=alice action=logout   ts=$(date -Iseconds)") | \
  enprot audit-log --signer auditor.pem access-log.ept</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      The resulting file has three chain anchors, each signing the file state with that line
      appended. Every anchor's parent is the previous one — a linear chain.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Verification (later)</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>enprot verify-chain \
    --trust-root $AUDITOR_FP \
    --strict-timestamps \
    access-log.ept</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      Any tampering — deleting a line, inserting one, modifying one — breaks the chain at that
      point. <code>verify-chain</code> reports OK or FAIL with the specific broken anchor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Capability framing</h2>
    <table class="w-full border border-enprot-ink/10 text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-enprot-ink/5">
        <tr><th class="p-2 text-left">Participant</th><th class="p-2 text-left">Holds</th><th class="p-2 text-left">Capability</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Logger</td><td class="p-2">auditor.pem (privkey)</td><td class="p-2 font-mono">Signer(auditor_fp)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Auditor</td><td class="p-2">auditor.pub</td><td class="p-2 font-mono">Verifier(auditor_fp)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Public</td><td class="p-2">nothing</td><td class="p-2 font-mono">Viewer</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      The public can see the log structure but can't tamper with it; the auditor can verify;
      only the logger can append.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Status</h2>
    <p>
      Specified in <a href="https://github.com/engyon/enprot/blob/main/TODO.finalize/27-audit-log-mode.md" class="text-enprot-accent hover:underline"><code>TODO.finalize/27</code></a>.
      Depends on Stage 1 (<a href="/docs/stages/1" class="text-enprot-accent hover:underline">chain DAG</a>) + Stage 4a (policy for <code>--strict-timestamps</code>).
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
